\f1\fs22 Gerry flipped to the next dossier, sliding it over.\
\'93The TPlusVirus?\'94 Dunhilton read, squinting at the label like it might bite.\
Gerry made his summary brisk, his voice tightening just a little. \'93Late in their program, Dr Hermann took the same viral platform, and instead of loading it with a killer payload, he loaded it with everything he thought made a human exceptional: tardigrade DNA, volcanic vent fungi, immortal jellyfish, anaerobic bacteria. The result\'97the TPlusVirus\'97optimises every cell in the host. Perfect memory, sight, healing, immunity. Bones denser, nerves sharper, lifespan up, everything.\'94\
\'93Tardigrades. The the little microscopic animal that look like gummy bears, right?\'94\
\'93They are called micro-animals.\'94\
Dunhilton snorted. \'93Does the virus cure cancer?\'94\
Gerry shrugged. \'93Possibly. It\'92s a significant life extender, and makes you functionally\'97for want of a better word\'97invincible. Unless, I guess, you fall from a window.\'94\
\'93How does it spread?\'94 Dunhilton said, glass halfway up. \'93Please don\'92t say networking meetings.\'94\
\'93Sex. Fluid contact.\'94\
Dunhilton exhaled, briefly relieved. \'93Well, that\'92s the missus out of danger,\'94 he muttered. \'93But Jamison Hardy sealed the labs, you said? With everyone still inside?\'94\
\'93Everyone who mattered, your Supreme Corporateness.\'94\
\'93So, presumably, doctors Hermann and Anstey.\'94\
Gerry nodded.\
Dunhilton weighed risks. \'93I still want the killer virus.\'94\
\'93Jamison Hardy saw fit to seal the labs.\'94\
\'93I hear you. The economy needs an underclass with long, drawn-out illnesses. Horrible ones. Expensive ones. Hardy must have known it would be bad for business; he knew what side of the bread was for the caviar. He was a true hero, a true Latter Day Capitalist. 
